WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.000
Will often talk on the show about how on so many&nbsp;
different issues the country, meaning the voters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:13.120
are as far left as they have been oftentimes for&nbsp;
as long as we've been keeping track of certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.000
metrics. So as an example will often say, well,&nbsp;
when you ask people, should abortion be legal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:25.120
in most cases, we have more approval for that.&nbsp;
Yes, abortion should be legal in most cases today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.720
than at any point during the Roe we Roe v&nbsp;
Wade era, which started decades and decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.880
ago. We talk about gay marriage, for example.&nbsp;
We'll talk about, well, should the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.960
provide some basic level of health care to&nbsp;
everybody, regardless of ability to pay?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:46.400
But another area in which public opinion&nbsp;
has shifted towards the left over the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.720
many years that does not get as much attention&nbsp;
is support for labor unions. Labor unions in

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:54.480
the United States are experiencing,&nbsp;
based on this brand new Gallup poll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.800
that came out under two weeks ago, which I did&nbsp;
not talk about, but I'm talking about today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:06.720
approval for labor unions in the United States&nbsp;
is at its highest point since 1965. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:14.720
57 years. And when you look at the numbers, you&nbsp;
now see that 68% of Americans currently approve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:21.600
of labor unions. That's a number that bottomed&nbsp;
out at 48% in 2009. That got close to 68%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.760
in the late nineties and early 2000. But you have&nbsp;
to actually have to go all the way back to 1965&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.200
where you see that support&nbsp;
for labor unions was at 71%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.160
Now, this is data that we have going back&nbsp;
a long way because Gallup has measured this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:42.640
support for labor unions going all the way back&nbsp;
to 1936. And there was a period in the United

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.000
States from 36 to 67, roughly, where&nbsp;
if you're looking at this chart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.720
you see this graph, you see that there was&nbsp;
just generally higher support in that roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:00.240
30 year period. You had an average of 68%&nbsp;
support for labor unions in the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.400
that dropped into the mid-to-high fifties for&nbsp;
a while and went even lower around the time of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.600
the Great Recession. But those numbers have now&nbsp;
recovered. And the interesting question would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:17.200
why? One part of this is when you break&nbsp;
down these numbers by party affiliation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.160
Democrats, Republicans, independents, it&nbsp;
won't shock you that support among Democrats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:29.440
is where labor union support is highest. But even&nbsp;
among Democrats, labor union support has also hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.000
a 20 year high. Labor union support&nbsp;
among Democrats has always been higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.360
in this entire period, but it is particularly&nbsp;
higher right now as well. And to understand

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.160
what's going on, you need only look at sort&nbsp;
of the world around us. In the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:53.840
people are increasingly understanding that we need&nbsp;
some counterbalancing power or influence against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:03:00.080
the power of corporations and against the power of&nbsp;
employers in the employee employer relationship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:08.560
This is a reaction to what is correctly being&nbsp;
seen out in the world the gig economy. More and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:15.600
more people realizing how absurd the disconnect&nbsp;
between productivity and wages has become. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.560
if you don't know that technically, even if you&nbsp;
don't have the data in front of you intuitively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:27.440
there is a sense of that. And it is true that on&nbsp;
some of these things, the pendulum sort of switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:32.080
swings back and forth and there are periods&nbsp;
where the country moves away from favoring labor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:38.080
unions to some degree and moves back in&nbsp;
favor of labor unions. The key is what

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:44.160
can you get done when the pendulum is swinging in&nbsp;
your favor? What can you accomplish when support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:52.000
is higher? I think it also should be mentioned&nbsp;
that President Joe Biden is arguably more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.160
pro-union, vocally so than any recent president.&nbsp;
I mean, certainly in the last 20 or so years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:05.440
And he talks about it as well. And Joe Biden's&nbsp;
background riding the Amtrak train. Amtrak also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:09.360
has been in the news for what appears to&nbsp;
be a narrowly averted major work stoppage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:16.160
a labor strike. Biden talks about that&nbsp;
certainly more than Trump. More than Obama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.880
of course, more than Bush. And I guess you&nbsp;
have to go back and think, well, is it sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:25.040
of like a Clinton level amount of talking about&nbsp;
labor unions? So none of this should shock us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:31.680
The optimistic thing is we often worry&nbsp;
and I talk about this on the show

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:38.640
all the time, that there's a disconnect&nbsp;
between what is happening in the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.640
and public opinion. So, for example,&nbsp;
you will see the Republican Party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:52.560
swing cartoonishly and absurdly into this&nbsp;
MAGA world where there is no more policy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:05:00.400
It's authoritarianism and sort of fascistic,&nbsp;
dictatorial, wet dreaming in terms of what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:06.640
would like to see while claiming to be about small&nbsp;
government. What? It just doesn't make any sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:11.520
And we sometimes see that the Republican Party&nbsp;
electorate or just voters in general, they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:16.240
see the scope of what is happening within the&nbsp;
Republican Party. And this ends up with, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.080
Herschel Walker might defeat Raphael Warnock&nbsp;
in Georgia. And it's horrible. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:25.120
we often are concerned that the people are not&nbsp;
reacting to the reality of what's going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:29.840
The good news here, if we want&nbsp;
to find some good news beyond

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:33.040
just oh, it's good that people&nbsp;
are more in favor of labor unions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:41.440
is that this is an accurate and logical reaction&nbsp;
to what is happening in terms of the relationships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.640
between employer and employee, in terms of the&nbsp;
disproportionate power that large corporations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:51.040
have, not only with regards to their employees,&nbsp;
but also with regard to taxation and so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.920
other things. So this is good to see. The&nbsp;
question now becomes what's going to be done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.000
about it? Will there actually be any steps&nbsp;
in terms of the democratization of work?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.720
Joe Biden has ideas that are going nowhere&nbsp;
naturally because of what's going on in the Senate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:09.280
right now in not having 60 votes. But we're going&nbsp;
to continue talking about it. And the first step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:13.040
in achieving change is public opinion&nbsp;
uniting, and we're seeing that to some degree.

